    <item>
      <title>Vagal afferent activation induces a dissociated NREM sleep-like state with wake-related &lt;i&gt;locus coeruleus&lt;/i&gt; activity</title>
      <link>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-79135-y</link>
      <content:encoded>
                &lt;p&gt;Nature Communications, Published online: 13 November 2026; &lt;a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-79135-y"&gt;doi:10.1038/s41467-026-79135-y&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;The role of peripheral signals in sleep state regulation is not well understood. Here authors show that vagal sensory afferent activation induces a NREMS-like state with low-frequency EEG, slowed heart rate, and cooling, while elevating locus coeruleus noradrenergic activity, revealing brain arousal within a sleep-like state.</content:encoded>
      <dc:title>Vagal afferent activation induces a dissociated NREM sleep-like state with wake-related &lt;i&gt;locus coeruleus&lt;/i&gt; activity</dc:title>
